frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Padouk Bb4, the printed Table I bar, cut solved in the engine, eta read from Fig. 4, no box, printed rubber mallet.
name	padouk-bb4-nobox-rubber-f4
source	Bar: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544: Bar No. 5 (G4, 397 Hz), xylophone CONCORDE X4001, padouk, L 29.3 cm, b 3.7 cm, h 1.9 cm edge; E, density, gamma_B through the packs named below. Eta 3.86e-7 s is read by us from Fig. 4 of the same paper (alpha = 6.11 + 7.62e-6 f^2, Eq. 19), 4.22 times Table I's 9.16e-8; we cannot say why the two differ and they may not be the same bar. Ratios: USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282, Honduras mahogany, stand-in.
note	NOT in the source: the undercut's shape on this note (solved here in the engine, a circular arc, reach 118.310 mm, middle 12.009 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament and f2 = 3 f1; the printed bar itself is tuned about 1:4 at 397 Hz, Table II; the 1:3 target is from xylophone tuning practice in print, not from the recording). No resonator is in this render. Mallet: the printed rubber row, 1.0 m/s, strike 15.9 cm, Table I and Fig. 8. Iowa's bars are not known to be padouk.
